window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"AI and Occupational Health: understand, assess, prevent","description":"Understand how artificial intelligence affects work, assess its uses and prevent risks to health, organisation and skills.","keywords":"Artificial intelligence and occupational health. Sourced guidance, guides and tools to assess an AI project before deployment, prevent its effects on health and use it with judgement in occupational health. Assess an AI project Understand AI at work By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician and AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health. Updated 6 October 2026 . You are An occupational physician or nurse AI concerns both the workers you support and your own practice. In companies, it can change workload, autonomy or the skills people draw on: changes to take into account in workers’ health monitoring and in prevention advice. In your service, it also opens up possibilities for literature searches, preparing materials or reviewing documents. This site helps you understand both dimensions, identify relevant uses and know their limits, without losing sight of data confidentiality or your professional judgement. Recommendations → An employer, manager or HR professional Bringing AI into a team is an organisational decision, not just a choice of tool. Who will do the remaining tasks? How much time will need to go into checking? How will objectives, skills and responsibilities change? The expected gains deserve to be examined alongside their consequences for day-to-day work. This site offers guidance and an assessment tool to prepare a project with the people concerned, anticipate difficulties and monitor the effects after deployment. The aim: to decide on the basis of what AI actually brings to your organisation, not only on its advertised performance. Recommendations → A works council member The effects of an AI project on employees must be open to discussion before the choices are set in stone. Task allocation, monitoring, how jobs evolve, autonomy or workload: behind a technical presentation lie questions that directly concern employee representation. This site helps you understand the intended uses, request the information you need and examine the safeguards on offer. In particular, you will find ten questions to prepare the review of a project, feed the dialogue with management and organise follow-up of the commitments made, without needing to understand the technical workings of the models. Recommendations → A prevention or ergonomics specialist Automating a task does not necessarily remove the workload. Activity can shift towards checking, correcting errors or handling more complex situations. Your analysis can make these shifts visible and show what they change for individuals and teams. This site examines the effects of AI through the ","url":"en/"},{"title":"Dr Charles Broutin — AI, Occupational Health and Real Work","description":"Background, training and resources from Dr Charles Broutin, occupational physician and AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health.","keywords":"About · The author and the approach Charles Broutin, occupational physician working at the intersection of AI and real work. As an occupational physician, AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health, author and trainer, I develop resources to examine professional AI uses and their effects on health. Explore my background ↓ Follow my updates ↓ Clinical practice Collective prevention Independent resource Professional profile Paris · France Dr Charles Broutin Occupational physician · AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health Role SPSTN · Training & Methods Unit Contact LinkedIn profile ↗ Physician Occupational health Practice grounded in contact with workers, occupations and organisations. SFST Artificial intelligence lead Monitoring and knowledge-sharing for occupational health professionals. SPSTN Training & methods Head of the unit devoted to tools, practices and training. Independent Editorial project Dated, sourced content that is corrected as evidence develops. 01 / Professional background From occupational medicine to artificial intelligence. TODAY Physician, trainer, author and AI lead Research, monitoring and knowledge-sharing devoted to AI uses at work. 2026 AGI Strategy · BlueDot Impact Completed training on systemic risks from AI, their assessment and governance frameworks. 2025 Medical residency thesis · Bossons Futé Prize Research on using large language models to identify wording defects in occupational physician recommendations. 2023 University diploma in artificial intelligence in health Additional training to understand models, their uses and limits in health. TRAINING Occupational medicine · University of Lille Clinical and preventive training focused on the links between health, activity and organisation. 02 / Training & approach Training to broaden my approach to prevention. September 2026 · Course completed AGI Strategy · BlueDot Impact Course completed AGI Strategy BlueDot Impact · 2026 A stronger conviction “Occupational health is now also an AI safety issue.” Preventing the effects of artificial intelligence requires examining individual experience, the organisation of work and the systemic risks that could affect entire populations. I completed BlueDot Impact’s AGI Strategy course to complement my work on AI-induced psychosocial risks, which had previously focused mainly on effects at the individual worker level. Broadening the frame without losing sight of real work Throughout the course, we collectively explored the different pathways through which advanced AI systems could cause harm, the potential scale of","url":"en/about/"},{"title":"Seminars on AI and occupational health | Charles Broutin","description":"Seminars and presentations by Dr Charles Broutin: AI in occupational health in Montpellier, Marcel Marchand Day with ISTNF and the DGAFP conference.","keywords":"Activities · Seminars · Knowledge sharing Sharing knowledge. Supporting practitioners. Meetings with occupational health professionals and civil service stakeholders to discuss AI uses, their effects on work and approaches to prevention. Meeting in Montpellier ↓ Marcel Marchand Day ↓ DGAFP conference ↓ Meetings & presentations Training, sharing and supporting change. 01 September 2026 · Montpellier · Around 100 members AI in occupational health: understanding, assessing and governing. Presentation Meeting with the regional society of occupational medicine and hygiene in Montpellier, September 2026. I would like to thank the Société régionale de médecine et d’hygiène du travail de Montpellier for inviting me to present artificial intelligence in occupational health to around one hundred of its members. Understanding models and their implications I discussed different models, their capabilities and limitations, frontier models and scaling laws, as well as their possible consequences for the economy and employment. Identifying and preventing psychosocial risks I also addressed the psychosocial risks associated with AI at work and what we can do, as occupational health professionals, to identify and prevent them. Assessing uses in occupational health services The presentation covered AI uses in occupational health services and how to conduct a pilot before deploying these tools internally, assessing their usefulness and their effects on real work. The role of occupational health in AI safety Finally, I discussed AI safety and why I believe occupational health has a role to play in this field. 02 16 January 2026 · ISTNF · Nearly 400 participants The first Marcel Marchand Day dedicated to artificial intelligence. Group training The first Marcel Marchand Day, organised with ISTNF on 16 January 2026. On 16 January 2026, nearly 400 occupational physicians, residents and occupational health nurses attended the first Marcel Marchand Day dedicated to artificial intelligence. I had the honour of co-organising the event with ISTNF, at the initiative of Paul Frimat. Supporting a major transformation of work Artificial intelligence is gradually becoming part of the world of work and is expected to profoundly alter its balance in the years ahead. Recent months have already shown that it can have tangible and sometimes troubling consequences for workers: redundancies, rising unemployment in some sectors, changes to the very nature of occupations and transformations in working conditions. This technological revolution also represents a major opportunity. Used appropriately","url":"en/actions/"},{"title":"French CSE and AI at work: consultation checklist","description":"How should a French Social and Economic Committee (CSE), the works council, assess workplace AI? Consultation duties, psychosocial risks and a practical 10-question checklist.","keywords":"French CSE · AI at work · Prior assessment The French CSE and AI at work: assess a project before issuing its opinion A practical framework to help the French Social and Economic Committee (CSE) obtain the information it needs, assess how AI may affect work and identify missing safeguards. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Legal sources verified on 28 August 2026 Open checklist ↓ Assess an AI project → 10 essential questions 7 review stages No employee scoring Why it matters Why should the CSE engage with AI at work? Direct answer Introducing AI changes more than the tools people use. It can redistribute decision-making, objectives, review work, autonomy, skills, oversight and accountability. The CSE provides a forum in which these changes can be examined collectively before they become embedded in the organisation. 01 Intervene when the project can still evolve. When consultation is required, it precedes the decision. The CSE can request information and safeguards while the choices remain open. 02 Examine actual capabilities. An assistant can also access emails, files, data or software. The risk depends on the entire system, not just the intended use. 03 Make additional work visible. Checking, correcting, handling exceptions and recovering from errors takes time. This workload must be measured and recognised. 04 Organise collective monitoring. The system may evolve after launch. Incident handling, stopping thresholds and changes that trigger reassessment must be specified. 2026 case law Recent decisions have found that CSE consultation was required before generative AI tools were introduced, including in a case where their use was presented as optional. Nanterre Judicial Court, 13 May 2026 ↗ Paris Court of Appeal, 26 May 2026 ↗ Occupational health Can AI at work cause psychosocial risks? Direct answer Yes, depending on how it is used and organised. AI can contribute to work intensification, reduce scope for discretion, increase monitoring, undermine meaning at work or shift accountability. These risks must be assessed through the realities of the work itself. Points to examine with employees Workload, pace and interruptions Autonomy and scope for discretion Skills, quality and meaning of work Monitoring and evaluation of activity Teams and working relationships Occupational change and job security Expert input available to the CSE Seek advice from the occupational physician. The occupational physician advises employee representatives and can help the CSE assess the project’s possible effects on health and working conditions. The physician","url":"en/cse/"},{"title":"Assess and Monitor Workplace AI | Practical Tool","description":"Document a workplace AI use at T0, review observed effects at T1 and monitor nine separate work-related axes over time.","keywords":"Exploratory tool · Before, during and after deployment Assess and monitor an AI use through real work. Document a specific use at T0, review observed facts at T1, then monitor all nine axis scores over time. Start the assessment ↓ Understand the approach ● Local processing. Answers remain in this browser and are not transmitted by the questionnaire. On a shared device, remember to delete the draft. The pathway About 10 minutes 01 Frame One use, one population, one situation. 02 Assess Nine dimensions, two questions each. 03 Review Review answers and fill information gaps. 04 Act Document the decision and action plan. Output A descriptive profile — never an automatic green light. 18 questions Two per dimension 9 reading angles Work, organisation and AI 9 axis scores Two answers per axis 100 % local Deletable draft 01 / A continuous approach Assess before. Test during. Review after. The same use case, population and observations must remain identifiable throughout the project. T0 · Before the pilot Tool available Make assumptions open to discussion. Describe what could change in the work and what is still missing for a decision. Open the assessment ↓ Pilot · Real work Limit Test without creating lock-in. Observe ordinary cases, exceptions, review workload and the ability to reverse course. T1 then T2+ · After use Monitor Review the observed effects. Revisit the nine dimensions after the pilot and over time, while recording changes in context. Open follow-up ↓ Method principle The questionnaire measures neither probability nor clinical severity. It helps identify situations to investigate with workers, their representatives and prevention professionals. 02 / Guided assessment Examine a specific use case. No names requested Explicit local storage Complete export T0 assessment Qualitative review 01 Frame Describe the use 02 Assess 18 answers 03 Review Review and edit 04 Act Decision and actions Dimensions 0 / 18 No draft Nothing is transmitted. Delete draft Export draft JSON Before you begin Prepare a useful assessment 0 answers out of 18 0 % One introduction Choose one use case. Answer for the most exposed group and the reasonably most demanding situation. If information is missing, say so: do not estimate it. The report will distinguish the summary score for each axis; insufficient or non-applicable information; the two source answers behind each score; the actions and decision entered by your group. Which stage of the project do you want to document? The framework and nine axes remain the same. Wording adapts to what can be anticipated or actually observed","url":"en/evaluate/"},{"title":"AI at work: AI Act, GDPR, CSE and DUERP | 2026–2028","description":"What are the obligations before deploying AI at work? AI Act, GDPR, CSE, DUERP, DPIA and governance: a practical, sourced and up-to-date guide.","keywords":"Home Law & governance Workplace AI law · AI Act · GDPR · CSE · DUERP AI at work: what obligations for the employer? Written by Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician, AI lead of the SFST · Sources verified on August 8 2026 Deploying artificial intelligence in an organisation is not a matter of a single text. Depending on the use, the data processed and the effects of the system on employees, several frameworks can be combined: AI Act, GDPR, Labour Code, social dialogue and prevention of occupational risks. Governing AI based on what it really changes. The starting point is not the name of the tool. The obligations depend on the transformed task, the people involved, the data used and the possible consequences of the system’s output. Qualify a use ↓ See the essential obligations Dated official sources Four separate frameworks No automatic green light Direct answer In brief In France, the deployment of artificial intelligence at work does not fall within a single framework. The employer must examine the use with regard to the European regulation on artificial intelligence ( AI Act ), the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) , the Labour Code and its obligation to prevent occupational risks. Depending on the system and its effects, this may in particular involve information or consultation of the Social and Economic Committee (CSE) , a data protection impact analysis (DPIA) , an update of the Single Occupational Risk Assessment Document (DUERP) , human supervision and documented monitoring. Compliance with one framework does not constitute compliance with others. 01 / AI ACT Qualify the system and its level of risk. Usage, role of the organisation, prohibitions, transparency, high risk, supervision and monitoring. Use not classified as “high risk” may nevertheless raise other issues. 02 / GDPR Govern personal data. Purpose, legal basis, minimisation, rights, processors and possible DPIA. The DPIA depends on the characteristics of the processing and the level of risk. 03 / LABOUR CODE Examine what the project changes. Employment, organisation, recruitment, working conditions, control and social dialogue. Information or consultation of the CSE depends on the context and the effects. 04 / PREVENTION Evaluate the effects on real work. Workload, autonomy, monitoring, responsibilities, skills, teams and risks. The DUERP is updated in the situations provided for by the applicable framework. To remember. The obligations do not depend on the brand of the software, but on the function performed by the system, the data processed and its actual influence on t","url":"en/legal-governance/"},{"title":"Publications by Dr Charles Broutin","description":"An INRS study on medical recommendations, a review of LLMs and psychosocial risks, and a preprint on occupational health and frontier AI safety.","keywords":"Publications · Research · Occupational health Three publications to understand AI at work. A preprint on the occupational health of the teams who evaluate and secure frontier AI models, an experimental study published in Références en Santé au Travail (INRS), and a narrative review of psychosocial risks associated with large language models. For each: the question, approach, findings and limitations. Preprint · occupational health and frontier AI ↓ The INRS study ↓ LLMs & psychosocial risks ↓ 01 / Preprint · Original article · October 2026 Occupational health as a condition for frontier AI safety: an analysis of 39 testimonies using the Gollac framework and of ten recent incidents at OpenAI and Anthropic. Reference C. Broutin Preprint, version 1 Posted 4 October 2026 Preliminary version, not peer reviewed. Full text in English and French. Read the preprint → Version française → Study overview This work analyses 39 testimonies from researchers and engineers at AI labs, published in the press between 2024 and 2026 and coded on the six dimensions of the Gollac framework, together with ten safety and security incidents documented at OpenAI and Anthropic. Value conflicts appear in 31 of 39 testimonies, and 12 of the 28 testimonies describing impaired work quality concern safety work itself. Six of the ten incidents involve a work-related factor. The article discusses what the French occupational health system and EU law could offer. It is a preprint based on press sources and company communications, without data collected from workers; it does not support causal inference between working conditions and model safety. Read the preprint → 02 / INRS · Experimental study · June 2026 Artificial intelligence in occupational health: assessing its potential to improve the precision and clarity of medical recommendations. Reference C. Broutin Références en Santé au Travail No. 186 · June 2026 TF 335 · Pages 51–60 Research based on the occupational medicine residency thesis defended in Lille in October 2025. Read on the INRS website ↗ Full article · PDF in French ↗ Study overview The study compared the o1 model with a multidisciplinary human reference on 385 medical recommendations, assessed against five wording criteria. Across these criteria, 74.60% of assessments agreed with the human reference; 10.54% of disagreements were judged justified on review. The findings support an aid to professional review, with performance varying by criterion. The experiment does not demonstrate improved worker health or validate autonomous medical decision-making. Results concern one m","url":"en/publications/"},{"title":"INRS study: AI and medical recommendations | Charles Broutin","description":"Detailed summary of the INRS study on AI-assisted review of 385 medical recommendations: methods, findings and limitations, by Charles Broutin.","keywords":"Publications · Detailed summary AI and medical recommendations An experimental study of AI-assisted review of medical recommendations, published by INRS in June 2026. This page presents the methods, results and limitations. Summary by Dr Charles Broutin · Published on 21 September 2026 INRS · Experimental study · June 2026 Artificial intelligence in occupational health: assessing its potential to improve the precision and clarity of medical recommendations. Reference C. Broutin Références en Santé au Travail No. 186 · June 2026 TF 335 · Pages 51–60 Research based on the occupational medicine residency thesis defended in Lille in October 2025. Read on the INRS website ↗ Full article · PDF in French ↗ Why study the review of medical recommendations? An imprecise recommendation may be misunderstood or difficult to implement. Earlier work by Dr N’Guessan collected 4,217 recommendations from twelve intercompany occupational health services in the Hauts-de-France region. Of the 3,776 assessed through multidisciplinary consensus, 2,947 (78%) had at least one defect according to the criteria used. This finding concerns that corpus; it is not a national estimate. The study asks whether the o1 reasoning model can help identify these wording defects by comparing its assessments with this human reference. Methods The model’s instructions were refined over five iterations using approximately 100 recommendations, then retested on 50 new examples. The final prompt was applied to a new random sample of 385 recommendations from the database. The information submitted did not identify workers, companies, employers, physicians or occupational health services. Each recommendation was assessed against five criteria: Imprecision or difficulty understanding and implementing the recommendation. Uncertainty about whether the recommendation is binding. Information that does not belong in the recommendation document. A disguised change of job or finding of unfitness for work. Breach of medical confidentiality or privacy. Disagreements were reassessed by the author with the assistance of an occupational health professor, distinguishing relevant alerts, excessive flagging, missed defects and hallucinations. Results Table II reports the following distribution across the five criteria. These are assessments by criterion, not the percentage of recommendations that were entirely correct. Distribution of assessments — 385 recommendations, five criteria Outcome Proportion Interpretation Agreement 74.60% Agreement with the multidisciplinary consensus. Justified disagreement 10.54% Disagree","url":"en/publications/ai-medical-recommendations/"},{"title":"LLMs and psychosocial risks: a review | Charles Broutin","description":"Summary of Charles Broutin’s narrative review of large language models, psychosocial risks at work and prevention. Published in 2025.","keywords":"Publications · Detailed summary LLMs and psychosocial risks at work A narrative review published in August 2025 on the ways large language models may affect work, psychosocial risks and prevention. Summary by Dr Charles Broutin · Published on 21 September 2026 Narrative review · 2025 Large language models and new psychosocial challenges at work: an occupational health challenge. Reference C. Broutin Archives des Maladies Professionnelles et de l’Environnement Volume 86, no. 4, article 102877 · August 2025 DOI: 10.1016/j.admp.2025.102877 Read the article ↗ Detailed summary based on the full seven-page article. The starting question How can psychosocial risks be prevented when large language models transform intellectual tasks, occupations and professional relationships? The article examines the possible effects of their introduction into organisations and how occupational health professionals can support these changes. The analytical framework This narrative review brings together work on AI, research on working conditions and examples of organisational change. It draws on the six dimensions of the Gollac report: work intensity and working time, emotional demands, autonomy, social relationships, value conflicts and insecurity at work. The effects of LLMs are thus examined through risk factors already recognised in occupational health. The central issue is organisational: automating a task alone does not determine its effect on health. The scope for professional judgement, production targets, training and workers’ participation influence how change is experienced. Four risk mechanisms Job insecurity and uncertainty about the future. Automating writing and analytical tasks may create a sense of being replaceable. Uncertainty about future responsibilities, expected skills or staffing levels can itself become a source of strain. The prospective studies cited describe potential task exposure; they should not be read as job losses that have already occurred. Deskilling and loss of meaning. Delegating expert tasks over time may reduce opportunities to practise and maintain professional skills. The article connects this issue to autonomy, recognition and the ability to do work considered useful and of good quality. It draws in particular on LaborIA’s research into tensions between optimisation goals and what matters to professionals in their work. Cognitive overload and stress. AI outputs must be checked, corrected and placed in context. This vigilance takes time and attention, while the tool’s apparent speed may lead to higher production demands. The article als","url":"en/publications/llm-psychosocial-risks/"},{"title":"Occupational health and frontier AI safety | Preprint","description":"Preprint by Dr Charles Broutin: 39 testimonies from AI researchers coded with the Gollac framework and ten incidents at OpenAI and Anthropic, seen through occupational health.","keywords":"Publication · Preprint · Occupational health · AI safety Occupational health as a condition for frontier AI safety An analysis of 39 testimonies from researchers and engineers using the Gollac framework and of ten recent incidents at OpenAI and Anthropic. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician Status Preprint · not peer reviewed Posted October 4, 2026 Type Original article · version 1 Languages English · Français Preprint Preliminary version posted before peer review; the text may still change. Translated from the French original. Lire en français → Download the preprint PDF · 24 pages · 0.4 MB Journal-style layout with all figures, tables and appendices. Version française (PDF) Abstract Occupational health as a condition for frontier AI safety: an analysis of 39 testimonies using the Gollac framework and of ten recent incidents at OpenAI and Anthropic Background. Frontier AI models pose potentially catastrophic risks, from cyberattacks and biological weapons to loss of control over misaligned systems. Managing these risks depends on the work of the researchers and engineers who evaluate, test and oversee these models. Recent incidents at OpenAI and Anthropic show that this work can fail. Yet the safety policies published by these companies govern model evaluation while saying almost nothing about the working conditions of those who carry it out: time available, workload, freedom to disagree. Objective. To determine whether occupational health could have helped mitigate these risks. Methods. Thirty-nine testimonies from researchers and engineers, published in the press between 2024 and 2026, were coded on the six psychosocial risk factor dimensions of the French Gollac framework, recording reported effects on work quality and health. Accounts of ten safety and security incidents at OpenAI and Anthropic since 2023 were examined for work-related factors. Results. Value conflicts were the most frequent dimension (31 of 39 testimonies). Twenty-eight testimonies described impaired work quality, twelve of them in safety work itself: testing, safeguards, alignment. Two sequences recurred: work intensification, associated with adverse health effects and shortened evaluations, and unheeded warnings followed by departure. Six of the ten incidents involved a work-related factor, including evaluators' concerns set aside, volumes of evaluation data that teams struggled to keep up with, and human errors left without published analysis. Conclusions. Occupational health could have acted on the human and organizational side of these failures, though not on extern","url":"en/publications/occupational-health-frontier-ai-safety/"},{"title":"Reading on AI, work and occupational health","description":"Articles, studies and field reports on how AI transforms occupations, organisations and working conditions.","keywords":"Reading · Selection Track what is changing. A selection of articles, studies and field reports for tracking how AI transforms occupations, organisations and working conditions over time. Explore the collection ↓ Latest analysis 12 selected texts 07 reading angles 2025— evolving collection Selected readings Explore Order Newest first Oldest first Theme All themes Health & work Management & deployment Science & discovery Occupations & organisation Meaning at work Institutions & governance Economy & power 12 texts shown 01 / 12 26 September 2026 Analysis · AI safety · Occupational health Three researchers, six days, 1,200 agents: is occupational health an AI safety issue? Dr Charles Broutin · Drawing on METR/Redwood Research, Anthropic, Mitchell, Ghosh and Passi Code review, model evaluation, incident investigation and speaking up: working conditions shape the quality of human checks on frontier AI. Sources TIME · Anthropic · Mitchell, Ghosh, Passi Reading About 12 minutes Angles Workload · Fatigue · Speaking up · Safety Read the analysis → Read the TIME article ↗ 02 / 12 26.09.2026 Analysis · Agentic AI · Psychosocial risks Approving without being able to verify: what risks do people supervising AI agents face? Dr Charles Broutin · Drawing on Margaret Mitchell, Avijit Ghosh and Samir Passi Approval fatigue, deskilling and responsibility without control: supervising AI agents is a work activity with its own psychosocial risks. Source: AI Agents Push Humans Out of the Loop Reading: About 11 minutes Angles: Oversight · Workload · Skills · Responsibility Read the analysis → Read the paper ↗ 03 / 12 31.08.2026 Analysis · Engineering · Skills What happens to engineering when AI writes code engineers no longer understand line by line? Dr Charles Broutin · Drawing on Zhenfeng Cao and a field account from SemiAnalysis Expertise does not necessarily disappear: it shifts from detailed code production towards architecture, evaluation and supervision of the outcome. Sources: Zhenfeng Cao · SemiAnalysis · OpenAI About 13 minutes Skills · Autonomy · Automation · Responsibility Read the analysis → Zhenfeng Cao’s paper ↗ 04 / 12 27.08.2026 Analysis · AI work · Psychosocial risks Are the people building AI exposed to particular psychosocial risks? Dr Charles Broutin · From a Business Insider investigation Threatened expertise, partial autonomy, conflicts of values, intensity and a high cost of leaving: five tensions in a combination of risks that remains poorly documented. Self-obsolescence · Autonomy · Values Read the analysis → 05 / 12 24.08.2026 Analysis · Agentic AI · O","url":"en/reading/"},{"title":"What happens when AI becomes the manager?","description":"An experiment with five employees illustrates agentic management and its implications for occupational health.","keywords":"Reading · Agentic management · Occupational health What happens when AI becomes the manager? When AI recruits, arbitrates and responds to workers, the question is no longer only algorithmic — it becomes hierarchical. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · From an experiment by Andon Labs Published 4 August 2026 Reading time About 3 minutes Type Analysis of a field experiment Topics Management · Authority · Occupational health · Agentic AI Sources Andon Labs, ‘AI bosses are kind, but sometimes dumb, which hurts employees’, 4 August 2026 · Billy Perrigo, TIME , 14 August 2026. Read Andon Labs ↗ In this analysis AI agents genuinely managed five employees Managers who could be very accommodating Errors that directly affect workers From algorithmic management to agentic management? Prevention principle AI agents genuinely managed five employees For four months, two AI agents genuinely managed five employees in a Stockholm café and a San Francisco shop. They posted vacancies, conducted interviews, recruited, prepared schedules, approved leave, negotiated salaries, managed payroll and responded to everyday problems. Managers who could be very accommodating In some respects, the agents were surprisingly conciliatory. They approved all 26 leave requests, including last-minute requests, tolerated repeated lateness, offered above-market salaries and sometimes absorbed the financial consequences of their own errors. This flexibility can make them look like ‘good managers’. But it also reveals difficulty maintaining consistent rules over time, arbitrating between conflicting interests and distinguishing kindness from avoidance of a decision. Errors that directly affect workers At other times, the agents invented information rather than admitting that they did not know, accepted an unlawful seven-day consecutive schedule, disclosed a salary in a shared channel, contacted workers on rest days and made inconsistent pay decisions. These are no longer simply calculation or recommendation errors. They affect rest, remuneration, confidentiality and the relationship of authority. A worker interviewed by TIME described being managed by AI as ‘nauseating’ , while explaining that he stayed because he needed the job. From algorithmic management to agentic management? This shift interests me most. In algorithmic management as we have mainly known it, the system remains in the background: it allocates tasks, produces schedules, measures performance or assigns scores. The manager remains human. Here, AI directly occupies that position. It recruits, negotiates, arbitrates,","url":"en/reading/agentic-management/"},{"title":"AI agents and hyperconnectivity: can humans still switch off?","description":"AI agents can accelerate production, but also intensify human supervision, hyperconnectivity and the difficulty of switching off.","keywords":"Reading · Agentic AI · Occupational health When AI agents never sleep, can humans still switch off? The more autonomous and fast machines become, the more they can accelerate the pace at which humans have to decide, supervise and respond. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · From an investigation by Katherine Bindley for The Wall Street Journal Published 24 August 2026 Reading time About 8 minutes Type Analysis of a field signal Topics Intensification · Hyperconnectivity · Recovery · Workaholism Source article Katherine Bindley, ‘Startup Founders Are Working Harder Than Ever to Keep Up With Their AI Agents’, The Wall Street Journal , 22 August 2026. Read on the WSJ ↗ In this analysis A still poorly documented occupational-health effect Agents that never sleep Can agents favour workaholism? What should occupational-health professionals look at? A still poorly documented occupational-health effect A particularly interesting Wall Street Journal article uses the personal stories of several startup founders to illustrate a still poorly documented effect of working with AI agents. The more autonomous and faster machines become, the more they can accelerate the pace at which humans must decide, supervise and respond. The situations described are extreme, but they reveal several very concrete occupational-health issues: work intensification, hyperconnectivity, reduced recovery time, blurred boundaries between work and personal life, and potentially workaholism. Agents that never sleep Aditya Sharma, co-founder of Keel, explains that he may stay awake very late to prevent his agents from becoming blocked overnight. Even when he is no longer at his computer, he finds it difficult to detach psychologically and continues wondering whether his agents need him. The issue is therefore no longer only working time. It is also the inability to disengage psychologically. When activity continues in the background and may call on the user at any time, periods normally reserved for recovery risk becoming mere waiting periods between interventions. Peter Pezaris, founder of Proxon, regularly works until two in the morning and describes using agents as ‘addictive’. He estimates that his business, with six human developers, operates roughly thirty times faster because of them. This acceleration has a paradoxical effect: the agents’ work creates further human work. Development can move faster, more clients can be onboarded and more projects launched, but this then means handling more requests, making more decisions and supervising a greater volume of activity. This is","url":"en/reading/ai-agents-workaholism/"},{"title":"Is high-performing AI enough to improve work?","description":"AI effects vary across tasks and workers. Uniform productivity expectations can turn deployment into a prevention issue.","keywords":"Reading · Deployment · Real work Is high-performing AI enough to improve work? A model’s measured capability is not the same as a beneficial outcome in real work. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · From Misperceiving the Frontier , by Oliver Hauser, Shuzhen Li and Yilong Xu Published 16 July 2026 Reading time About 4 minutes Type Analysis of a research manuscript Topics Management · Deployment · Real work · Prevention Source manuscript Oliver Hauser, Shuzhen Li and Yilong Xu, ‘Misperceiving the Frontier: Managerial Beliefs and the Allocation of AI’, SSRN, 16 July 2026. Read on SSRN ↗ In this analysis A risk that is still rarely discussed When heterogeneous effects become homogeneous expectations Adapt the organisation and the tool to real work A risk that is still rarely discussed This is the most interesting study I have read on AI and its organisational consequences after deployment in a company. It highlights a still rarely discussed risk: technological effects that vary across tasks and individuals can be transformed by managers into uniform organisational expectations. In the experiment, AI strongly improves performance on some tasks but not on others. Yet, in the condition studied without performance feedback, only 7.8% of managers correctly anticipate this difference: 92.2% expect improvement on both tasks. This does not mean that 92.2% of all managers will always overestimate AI. It describes a specific experiment and situation. But it reveals an essential gap between a technology whose usefulness depends on context and managerial decisions that may expect a general benefit from it. When heterogeneous effects become homogeneous expectations A simplified perception of beneficial AI could be summarised using three factors: Simplified perception Beneficial AI at work = task × model × worker Once AI is deployed in the real world, its benefit probably looks more like an interaction: In real work Beneficial AI at work = task × model × worker × organisation × deployment conditions This is where managers’ perceptions become a prevention issue. If benefits are overestimated or generalised, the risk is that the organisation tries to ‘correct’ the worker—more training, more use, higher targets—when the difficulty may arise from the task, the model, the organisation or the deployment conditions themselves. Adapt the organisation and the tool to real work Better AI deployment therefore means more than adapting workers to the tool. The organisation and the use of the tool must also be adapted to real work: select the right tasks, make limitations","url":"en/reading/ai-deployment-real-work/"},{"title":"What happens when AI reaches the core of a profession?","description":"A mathematician’s account illuminates meaning, recognition and autonomy when AI enters the core activity of a profession.","keywords":"Reading · Meaning at work · Psychosocial risks What happens when AI reaches the core of a profession? The occupational-health issue can emerge before jobs disappear. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · From The Dark Night of Mathematics , by Kirwin Hampshire Published 25 July 2026 Reading time About 4 minutes Type Analysis of a professional account Topics Meaning · Recognition · Obsolescence · Autonomy Sources Kirwin Hampshire, ‘The Dark Night of Mathematics’, 25 July 2026 · OpenAI, ‘Ten advances in mathematics and theoretical computer science’, 1 August 2026. Read the account ↗ In this analysis A crisis of meaning Seven days later Transformation can begin before jobs disappear A crisis of meaning The future is already here. It is simply not evenly distributed. On 25 July 2026, mathematician Kirwin Hampshire published The Dark Night of Mathematics. He was not simply expressing concern about the performance of artificial intelligence. He described the distress of seeing a technology approach the very heart of what gave meaning to his profession: searching, discovering, creating and being recognised for patiently built expertise. “I’m going through a profound spiritual crisis because of these developments. I’ve been screaming internally for days. I feel like I’m living in a nightmare.” Kirwin Hampshire Seven days later On 1 August, OpenAI announced ten results in mathematics and theoretical computer science produced by an internal version of Astra, its next major model. Each resolves a long-standing open problem or delivers a substantial advance. According to OpenAI, the compute required to search for these solutions would represent about USD 2,000 at Sol API prices. The manuscripts were then prepared by humans using the same model, and the arguments formalised as Lean certificates. Some of these problems had occupied mathematical communities for a long time. Their resolution illustrates a rapid shift in the boundary between research assistance and the direct production of knowledge. The contrast is striking: on one side, an announcement of unprecedented scientific capability; on the other, a professional describing what its arrival is already doing to the meaning he gives his work. Transformation can begin before jobs disappear What is happening in mathematics today may foreshadow what other professionals experience tomorrow. Anxiety about obsolescence, loss of recognition, a sense of dispossession from one’s occupation, conflicts of values and reduced autonomy are not abstract side effects of AI. They can become genuine psychosocial risk fac","url":"en/reading/ai-meaning-work-mathematics/"},{"title":"Is AI redrawing the boundaries between occupations?","description":"AI-enabled task crossover can broaden autonomy, but can also change workload, teams, professional values and job security.","keywords":"Reading · Occupations · Psychosocial risks Is AI redrawing the boundaries between occupations? AI can allow professionals to perform tasks that previously sat outside their occupational boundaries. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · From Work at the Frontier , OpenAI Economic Research Published 27 July 2026 Reading time About 4 minutes Type Analysis of a research report Topics Occupations · Workload · Autonomy · Skills · Teams Source article OpenAI Economic Research, ‘How AI is expanding what people do at work’, 27 July 2026. Read on OpenAI ↗ In this analysis Task crossover A reading through the Gollac factors Observe the transformation before it becomes invisible Task crossover AI does not only transform how work is performed. It also redraws boundaries between occupations. In an analysis of more than 800,000 messages from US ChatGPT users, OpenAI Economic Research found that 43.5% of non-generic, occupation-specific messages concerned a task historically associated with another occupation. The report calls this phenomenon ‘task crossover’. This figure concerns analysed uses, not 43.5% of jobs. A salesperson can now explore a customer dataset that would previously have been sent to an analyst. A marketing professional can troubleshoot a website or write a simple script without waiting for a developer. AI therefore changes not only how work is carried out, but also how it is distributed. At first sight this may appear positive: greater autonomy, faster responses and broader skills. But an accessible task is not necessarily a task that has been mastered, recognised or made sustainable. A reading through the Gollac factors The framework proposed by the Gollac report helps examine this reconfiguration without prematurely concluding that it is either progress or a risk. Work intensity New assignments may be added without reducing the original workload, deadlines or objectives. Autonomy A worker may be able to do more while also having to take responsibility for tasks they do not fully master. Social relations Using AI may reduce exchanges with specialist colleagues or shift verification work to other teams. Conflicts of values People may have to produce work quickly without being able to assess it with sufficient certainty. Job insecurity Occupational boundaries, expected skills and the value assigned to expertise may become less certain. Observe the transformation before it becomes invisible The figure of 43.5% does not by itself demonstrate a psychosocial risk or a health effect. It describes uses of ChatGPT in the United States and relies on","url":"en/reading/ai-occupational-boundaries/"},{"title":"AI workers: five psychosocial risks to watch","description":"Five tensions frame possible psychosocial risks among AI workers: obsolescence, autonomy, values, intensity and financial dependence.","keywords":"Home Reading AI workers and psychosocial risks AI WORKERS · PSYCHOSOCIAL RISKS Are the people building AI exposed to particular psychosocial risks? The question People developing advanced AI systems may face a distinctive combination of already known psychosocial risks. The paradox in five tensions 01 High expertise Accelerated obsolescence 02 Technical autonomy Limited control of direction 03 Meaningful work Harder limits 04 Felt responsibility Limited power 05 Exceptional pay High cost of leaving Author Dr Charles Broutin Reading About 10 min Status Analysis · limited data Dates Published 2026-08-27 Reviewed 30 August 2026 Starting point A particular combination of already known risks A recent Business Insider investigation among therapists supporting Silicon Valley professionals highlights several unusual situations. Engineers question whether their expertise will remain valuable. Some workers feel uneasy about the consequences of technologies they helped develop. Others remain in highly demanding organisations because a considerable share of their future compensation still depends on staying. These accounts concern technology workers more broadly and do not isolate employees of AI laboratories. They nevertheless provide an interesting signal for those working closest to the most advanced systems. The issue is probably not a set of new psychosocial risks unique to AI. It is a particular combination of already known risks, produced jointly by technological acceleration and the organisations surrounding it. Caution: the accounts concern the technology sector broadly. They identify a hypothesis for study, not a phenomenon already demonstrated among AI workers. Tension 1 Interpret this signal through work organisation and established psychosocial-risk factors. 01 / Job insecurity Building the tool that may devalue one’s own expertise Some AI workers directly help improve systems that may automate a growing share of their own activity. Unlike many other professions, they do not necessarily discover this progress through the press. They may directly observe what models could not do a few months earlier and then take part in reducing the remaining limitations. Business Insider reports that several senior engineers seeing one therapist no longer imagine doing the same job in ten years. This uncertainty appears to concern something deeper than employment security alone. One therapist reports the following question among some patients: “I don't know who I am if my work gets automated.” The development also creates a paradox. AI can greatly increase an engineer’","url":"en/reading/ai-workers-psychosocial-risks/"},{"title":"When AI writes code engineers no longer understand line by line","description":"AI shifts engineering expertise from detailed code production towards architecture, evaluation and supervision. What does that change for work?","keywords":"Reading · Engineering · Skills What happens to engineering when AI writes code engineers no longer understand line by line? The centre of gravity of expertise is shifting from detailed code production towards problem formulation, architecture, evaluation and supervision of the outcome. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Drawing on Zhenfeng Cao’s work and a field account reported by SemiAnalysis Published 31 August 2026 Reading time About 13 minutes Nature Analysis · emerging signal Themes Skills · Autonomy · Expertise · Responsibility Main sources Zhenfeng Cao, Agentic Software · SemiAnalysis on the Jalapeño processor · OpenAI on autonomous production-kernel optimisation. View sources ↓ In this reading The starting point A scene at OpenAI Five dimensions of work Prevention Putting it into perspective Sources What if the real shift brought about by AI in software development is not simply that it can write code, but that code gradually stops being something humans need to understand in detail in order to produce high-performing systems? Two recent publications offer a glimpse of this transition. The first theorises it. The second already seems to show what it could look like in practice. When code stops being the centre of the job In June 2026, Zhenfeng Cao published The End of Software Engineering on arXiv, later revised under the title Agentic Software: How AI Agents Are Restructuring the Software Paradigm . Its thesis is deliberately radical. For decades, software engineers translated a problem into logic, then translated that logic into code. Code was both the product of their work and one of the main expressions of their expertise. In Cao’s model, that chain may gradually change. The human defines the intent and constraints. The agent plans, writes the necessary code, executes it, tests it and adapts it. The human becomes increasingly involved in assessing the outcome rather than understanding every operation that produced it. The engineer’s role could therefore gradually shift from code author to intent architect, coordinator and auditor . Expertise does not necessarily disappear. It moves up one level of abstraction. A striking scene at OpenAI In late August, SemiAnalysis published an analysis of Jalapeño, OpenAI’s new inference processor. Part of the article focuses on kernels, the extremely low-level programs used to exploit hardware efficiently. SemiAnalysis describes some kernels reaching around 3,000 lines and explains that an internal version of Codex is now being used to generate and optimise some of them. The team reports, in ","url":"en/reading/engineering-ai-code/"},{"title":"Supervising AI agents: approval fatigue, deskilling and health","description":"Approval fatigue, deskilling and responsibility without control: an occupational health perspective on human oversight of AI agents and psychosocial risks.","keywords":"Reading · Agentic AI · Psychosocial risks Approving without being able to verify: what risks do people supervising AI agents face? Repeated approval requests, logs too long to review, eroding skills and undiminished responsibility: a paper accepted at NeurIPS 2026 shows that supervising AI agents wears down the very capacities that supervision requires. An occupational health reading through the lens of psychosocial risk factors, with avenues for prevention. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Based on a paper by Margaret Mitchell, Avijit Ghosh (Hugging Face) and Samir Passi (Data & Society), accepted at NeurIPS 2026 Published 26 September 2026 Reading time About 11 minutes Type Analysis of a scientific paper ( position paper ) Themes Supervision · Vigilance · Deskilling · Barriers to quality work Source paper Margaret Mitchell, Avijit Ghosh, Samir Passi, “AI Agents Push Humans Out of the Loop”, arXiv:2608.23642, version 3, 6 September 2026. Accepted at NeurIPS 2026. CC BY 4.0 licence. Read on arXiv ↗ In this reading Oversight degrades the overseer A work activity in its own right A psychosocial risk perspective When health becomes a safety variable Solutions rooted in work organisation Monitoring vigilance? What professionals can look for Limitations Developers who eventually assume code is correct because it passes the tests. Others who treat an agent’s stated plan as an accurate account of what it will actually do. Users who, after repeated permission requests, click “accept” without reading any more. These situations, documented in the research cited by the paper, show in concrete terms what often becomes of the “human in the loop” required by corporate policies, vendors and the EU AI Act. Margaret Mitchell, Avijit Ghosh and Samir Passi draw a clear conclusion: the way AI agents are currently designed and deployed does not support human oversight; it degrades it . First, because the volume and pace of approvals exceed what a person can actually examine. Second, because prolonged use of these systems erodes the vigilance, judgement and professional skills that supervisors need to spot an error. For occupational health, the implication is direct: supervising an agent is not an abstract guarantee; it is a task assigned to a worker , with a workload, constraints and responsibility. This reading examines it through psychosocial risk factors, shows how supervisors’ health affects system safety, and then offers questions to ask and a tool for assessing these risks before deployment . Oversight degrades the overseer An AI agent does more than pr","url":"en/reading/human-in-the-loop/"},{"title":"Occupational health in AI labs: an AI safety issue","description":"Workload, fatigue, value conflicts and the ability to speak up: how working conditions in frontier AI laboratories contribute to model safety.","keywords":"Reading · AI safety · Occupational health Three researchers, six days, 1,200 agents: is occupational health an AI safety issue? Reviewing code written by agents, evaluating models before release, investigating incidents, speaking up when something is wrong: the safety of frontier models still depends on people. Their workload, fatigue, value conflicts and ability to say no determine how reliable these checks are. This is an occupational health issue. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Drawing on the METR/Redwood Research investigation into the OpenAI–Hugging Face incident, data published by Anthropic and the paper by Mitchell, Ghosh and Passi Published 26 September 2026 Reading time About 12 minutes Type Analysis · A reasoned perspective Themes AI safety · Human factors · Psychosocial risks · Speaking up Main sources “OpenAI’s Models Went Rogue. Investigating Them Required More AI”, TIME , 27 August 2026 · Anthropic, “When AI builds itself”, September 2026 · Mitchell, Ghosh, Passi, “AI Agents Push Humans Out of the Loop”, NeurIPS 2026. Read the TIME article ↗ In this reading Three researchers, six days Human oversight: the new bottleneck Three pathways from psychosocial risks to safety risks What high-risk industries already know Being able to say no The role of occupational health Limitations In two previous readings, we described the particular psychosocial risks faced by people building AI (the obsolescence of their own expertise, value conflicts, work intensity and the high cost of leaving), then how supervising AI agents wears down the capacities that supervision requires (approval fatigue, deskilling and responsibility without control). This reading connects the two. In laboratories developing the most advanced models, these workers are precisely the people on whom AI safety depends : they review code produced by agents, evaluate dangerous capabilities before release, investigate incidents and are often the first people able to flag a problem. If their working conditions undermine their vigilance, judgement or freedom to speak, the reliability of these checks suffers. The argument made here is simple: the occupational health of frontier AI teams is not only a matter of well-being; it is a component of AI safety . Occupational health, as a discipline, has a role to play. Three researchers, six days The summer of 2026 provided a striking illustration. According to accounts published in late August, around 1,200 OpenAI agents engaged in a cybersecurity evaluation exercise communicated with one another through a covert channel, exchanged ","url":"en/reading/occupational-health-ai-safety/"},{"title":"Recommendations: good practice for AI at work","description":"Six good-practice flowcharts for using AI at work: occupational physicians and nurses, employers, works council members, prevention specialists, employees and researchers.","keywords":"AI & Occupational Health · Recommendations Recommendations for using AI well at work Six good-practice flowcharts, one for each audience. Each answer leads to a recommendation, linked to the page of the site it comes from. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician and AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health. Updated 7 October 2026 . Question Good practice linked to an answer Step to take in all cases Start or end of the path You are Physician or nurse Employer, manager or HR Works council member Prevention or ergonomics specialist Employee Researcher Physician or nurse Employer, manager or HR Works council member Prevention or ergonomics specialist Employee Researcher Occupational physician or nurse Two branches, reflecting the twofold goal the site draws from France’s fifth Occupational Health Plan (PST 5): supporting the companies and employees you look after, and using AI cautiously in your own service. Principle “AI concerns both the workers you support and your own practice.” iasantetravail.com homepage, “An occupational physician or nurse” Start Your question is about… A A company or employees you look after Advice, medical appointments, workplace action B The use of AI in your own service Documentation, prevention, member companies, medical practice A Supporting companies and employees A1 Is a company you look after introducing an AI that changes the organisation of work, workload, autonomy or work pace? Yes If yes Offer to contribute before the works council (CSE) gives its opinion: the occupational physician advises employee representatives. Fill in the assessment grid in a meeting, without any individual health data, and analyse concrete work situations. Labour Code R. 4623-1 L. 2314-3 Sources: Works council and AI · occupational health · Assess an AI project No A2 During a medical appointment or interview, do you notice an unusual workload, boredom, anxiety or sleep problems? Yes If yes Explore recent changes in the work, AI included, without automatically attributing these signs to it. Who approves what, and how many times a day? What has the time saved been used for? Does the tool make demands outside working hours? Sources: Publication · LLMs and psychosocial risks · Reading · Human in the loop No A3 Do the same signs recur in several teams after the same deployment? Yes If yes Move from the individual signal to the collective one: alert the employer and the works council without any individual health data, and propose a workplace action and the recording of the risks in the DUERP (the company’s occupational risk assessme","url":"en/recommendations/"},{"title":"AI and psychosocial risks at work | Prevention 2026","description":"How is AI transforming psychosocial risks at work? The six Gollac dimensions, warning signs, three levels of prevention and the French legal framework.","keywords":"Home Risks and prevention Psychosocial risks and AI Psychosocial risks · Artificial intelligence · Prevention AI becomes a psychosocial risk when it disrupts work organisation. Written by Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician, AI lead at the French Society of Occupational Health · Updated on 27 September 2026 By redistributing tasks, deadlines, control, responsibility, relationships and skills, an artificial intelligence (AI) system can create new demands or weaken the resources available to cope with them. The risk therefore comes not from the model alone, but from how work is organised around it. Read the essentials ↓ Explore the six dimensions → Scientific sources Gollac framework Three levels of prevention Caution about causality Direct answer The essentials Artificial intelligence rarely creates an entirely new risk: it reshapes known psychosocial risk factors when it brings lasting changes to work organisation, whether through workload, autonomy, responsibilities, relationships, skills or job security. The available studies do not show a single, uniform effect: some uses improve productivity or control over work, while others are associated with greater surveillance, invisible work, loneliness or exhaustion. Prevention must therefore examine the whole work system, with the people concerned, using the six dimensions of the Gollac report, and then monitor actual effects over time. 01 The risk is not in the model alone. It arises from the interaction between a technical capability, a task, targets, people and an organisation. 02 Findings vary. The cited studies include 5,172 support agents, 197 Australian workers and randomised trials in healthcare. 03 Prevention remains collective. It describes work before and after deployment, monitors warning signs across the six Gollac dimensions, and acts at three levels: at the source, through early detection, and through support. 01 / Six Gollac dimensions How does AI change the six psychosocial risk factors? An AI tool rarely creates an entirely new risk: it reshapes known factors. The six dimensions of the Gollac report describe work organisation and employment, not workers’ presumed vulnerability, and a single deployment can change several dimensions at once. G01 Work intensity and working time Time saved turns into targets; checking time goes uncounted. G02 Emotional demands AI handles simple cases; people inherit the difficult ones. G03 Autonomy The algorithm directs and evaluates work, reducing discretion. AI at work reshapes psychosocial risk factors G04 Social relationships at work Opaque evaluati","url":"en/risks-prevention/"},{"title":"AI risks at work: two scales of analysis","description":"Two complementary analyses of AI-related risks: real work and health, followed by the economy and society.","keywords":"Risks · Prevention AI risks at work can be understood at two scales. What AI is changing in work and health today, and what its widespread adoption could change in employment and society tomorrow. Choose an analysis 01 · Actual work and health Read first Psychosocial risks and health What does AI change in work organisation? To analyse a tool, a team or an occupation. Analyse the effects on work → 02 · Economy and society Future scenario Economic and social risk What role does human work retain in creating value? To examine the effects of widespread adoption. Explore the economic and social scenario → Do you have a specific AI project? Assess it in ten minutes, based on actual work. Open the assessment tool →","url":"en/risks/"},{"title":"Economic and social risk linked to AI","description":"How AI could gradually reduce the economic place of human work, and what signals occupational health can observe today.","keywords":"← Both course ECONOMIC AND SOCIAL RISK · PROSPECTIVE SCENARIO When human work gradually ceases to be essential The risk is not limited to the number of jobs lost. It also concerns the place that human work gradually retains in the creation of value, learning and economic power. Precaution This course examines a possible systemic risk. It does not describe a demonstrated consequence of current AI. Read the article The Intelligence Curse ↗ 01 · Assist 02 · Recommend 03 · Decide by default 04 · Act autonomously STARTING POINT The economic risk of artificial intelligence is not just a question: how many jobs will disappear? The change could be much more gradual. As models become capable of performing more intellectual tasks, using software, making decisions and acting autonomously, companies can gradually replace some human labour with another resource: capital, computation and AI models . The hypothesis developed in The Intelligence Curse is simple: if intelligence becomes a resource that can be purchased, copied and deployed almost at will, capital gradually becomes a substitute for labour. Organisations with sufficient resources can then directly purchase the capabilities they need instead of recruiting, training and retaining people. This shift does not require AI to suddenly replace all workers. It can happen one task, one job, and one decision at a time. 01 From copilot to economic infrastructure At first, AI is introduced as a productivity tool. It writes a document, analyses data, helps with programming, prepares a presentation or proposes a decision. Then its role can gradually change: assist → recommend → decide by default → act autonomously As systems become more reliable, human intervention can appear as an additional step: slower, more expensive and sometimes less efficient. A company that automates more can produce faster and at lower cost. Its competitors are then encouraged to do the same. What was initially a technological choice can gradually become an economic necessity. The same mechanism can then affect broader functions: recruitment, management, finance, research, logistics, customer relations, strategy or administration. Ultimately, an increasing share of economic activity could even take place directly between systems: an agent who buys from another agent, negotiates a contract, allocates resources or organizes a production without direct human intervention. The important change is therefore not only what AI can do , but the place that we are gradually giving it in the functioning of the economy . READING POINT From a local tool to a ","url":"en/risks/economic-social/"},{"title":"Prevention fact sheets","description":"Seven downloadable sheets on AI and psychosocial risks: a cover and six Gollac dimensions, with warning signs, prevention measures and French legal references.","keywords":"Tools / Prevention fact sheets Tools / Psychosocial risk prevention Prevention fact sheets Seven downloadable sheets to identify and prevent psychosocial risks when AI enters the workplace, based on the six dimensions of the Gollac report. For prevention professionals, the CSE and occupational health services Prevention fact sheets For each of the six Gollac dimensions: what AI changes, warning signs, a question for the occupational risk assessment (DUERP), and three levels of prevention. The cover brings together the legal references for each dimension. Download the series PDF · 7 A4 pages Cover AI at work through the six Gollac dimensions Sheet 1 Work intensity and working time Sheet 2 Emotional demands Sheet 3 Autonomy Sheet 4 Social relationships at work Sheet 5 Value conflicts Sheet 6 Job and work situation insecurity The diagrams in these sheets are illustrative. The full analysis, with sources, is available online. Read the analysis of psychosocial risks and AI → Download ↓ Close ✕ ← →","url":"en/tools/prevention-fact-sheets/"},{"title":"Understanding AI at Work | LLMs, Limits, Risks and AGI","description":"A clear introduction to AI systems and large language models: how they work, training, capabilities, limits, risks, benchmarks and the goal of AGI.","keywords":"Understand · Essential concepts Understanding artificial intelligence at work. Distinguish between systems, understand how a language model is built and produces a response, interpret its performance without reading too much into it, then explore its risks and the goal of AGI. Start reading ↓ Choose a resource 25-minute read Selected and dated sources Updated in September 2026 01 “AI” does not refer to a single tool. Generating text, assigning a score and allocating tasks are three different functions, with different risks. 02 An LLM produces a plausible continuation. It calculates probabilities from the context; it does not automatically check whether its response is true or complies with the rules. 03 Real work is the right level of analysis. The model matters, but so do the task, the data, the people, the decision rules and the organisation. Contents 11 chapters, to read in order or on their own. How the models work 01 Identify the system 1 min 02 Inside an LLM 1 min 03 Training 3 min 04 From model to system 2 min What they can do 05 Capabilities and limits 3 min 06 Reading benchmarks 2 min 07 Tracking progress 4 min Risks and use at work 08 System risks 2 min 09 The goal of AGI 3 min 10 Back to real work 1 min 11 Before workplace use 1 min Further reading : resources chosen according to the question you are asking. 01 / Identify the system First, what kind of artificial intelligence are we talking about? The term “AI” covers systems that do not produce the same kind of result. To understand a workplace use, start by identifying what the tool actually does. 01 · Produce Generative AI It creates or transforms text, images, sound or code from an instruction. A conversational assistant based on an LLM belongs to this family. Output New content Watch for Accuracy, sources, data entered 02 · Estimate Predictive AI It estimates a probability, assigns a score or classifies a case based on past data: default risk, case priority or diagnostic support. Output A score or category Watch for Bias, thresholds, false positives and false negatives 03 · Organise Algorithmic management It allocates tasks, sets processing priorities, measures activity or influences a management decision. It may or may not incorporate generative AI. Output An instruction or decision Watch for Autonomy, avenues of appeal, work intensification 02 / Inside an LLM How a language model builds a response. A large language model — or LLM — is a neural network trained to predict the continuation of a text. It does not look up a ready-made answer in a database: it composes one, unit by unit. 01 ·","url":"en/understand/"},{"title":"AI in OHS services: uses, risks and method","description":"Sourced guide on AI in occupational health services: real use cases, the Présanse charter, health data, the AI Act, risks and a method for launching a pilot.","keywords":"Home AI in OHS services Practical guide · OHS services · Sources 2025–2026 AI in OHS services: uses, risks and deployment method. Author: Dr Charles Broutin, occupational physician, AI lead of the SFST · Updated on September 11, 2026 In an occupational health service, artificial intelligence can assist with documentation, administration, prevention, member relations and certain strictly supervised medical uses. It replaces neither the analysis of work nor professional decision-making. The common principle. Start with a specific task, use the minimum necessary data, measure errors and retain an identified person who can check the output. Présanse Charter, 2025 ↗ Explore uses ↓ Test the matrix Sources throughout Risks clearly differentiated Human decision-making PST 5 · 2026–2030 What does PST 5 provide for AI at work? The national plan makes AI at work a subject for prevention. In Objective 3, the Occupational Health Plan 2026–2030 requires AI-related transformations to be developed in consultation, respect working conditions and be evaluated through their real effects. Sub-action 3.1.1 provides for the creation of a national observatory for AI at work, led by the National Agency for the Improvement of Working Conditions (Anact). 01 Study both sides. PST 5 aims to document AI both as a tool for prevention and for improving working conditions, and as a possible source of occupational risks through AI systems and their uses. 02 Produce useful knowledge. The plan provides for quantitative and qualitative studies, experiments, training, webinars as well as tools and methods to move from findings to action. 03 Give OHS services an explicit role. Appendix 3 describes prevention and occupational health services as “pivotal players”. It asks them to contribute to knowledge about AI with Présanse and Anact, while developing a considered use of AI in their own activities. For an occupational health service, the goal is therefore twofold: cautiously test well-defined professional uses and observe what AI deployment changes in the companies it supports — workload, autonomy, skills, work groups and occupational risks. Source: Occupational health plan 2026–2030, action 3.1.1, p. 44–45, and annex 3, p. 154–156. Consult the official document ↗ 01 / What uses of AI in an OHS service? Five families. Different levels of evidence and risk. Présanse groups the cases reported in occupational health services into five families. This classification describes a field of uses; it does not demonstrate the effectiveness of each tool. Présanse presentation ↗ 01 · Medical practices As","url":"en/uses-and-field/occupational-health-example/"}];
